Subject: [PATCH 14/27] Formal-core Phase 2: interpret the valuation horizon T

T = the planning horizon over which a one-time cost is amortized, a
bounded-rationality stand-in for discounting.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 paper/tbv_mdr_Paper01-v04.tex | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/paper/tbv_mdr_Paper01-v04.tex b/paper/tbv_mdr_Paper01-v04.tex
index 7f4f37c..a873339 100644
--- a/paper/tbv_mdr_Paper01-v04.tex
+++ b/paper/tbv_mdr_Paper01-v04.tex
@@ -318,7 +318,7 @@ U_i(\sigma\mid\mu_i,q_i)=\sum_{C\in\mathcal P_i}\rho(C)\sum_{m\in M(\mathcal P_i
 \label{eq:subjective-value}
 \end{equation}
 where $\rho(C)=\sum_{s\in C}\rho(s)$.
-Agent $i$ values a lumpy, one-time cost against the gain it produces over a fixed \emph{valuation horizon} of $T$ periods, common across agents, without discounting.
+Agent $i$ values a lumpy, one-time cost against the gain it produces over a fixed \emph{valuation horizon} of $T$ periods, common across agents, without discounting---the planning horizon over which a one-time cost is amortized, a bounded-rationality stand-in for discounting.
 Revising the standing policy is costly: agent $i$ replaces $\sigma_i$ by $\sigma_i^{\star}$ if and only if the horizon-$T$ gain clears a one-time \emph{switching hurdle} $\kappa_i\ge0$,
 \[
 T\bigl(U_i(\sigma_i^{\star}\mid\mu_i,q_i)-U_i(\sigma_i\mid\mu_i,q_i)\bigr)>\kappa_i,
